---
/* =========================================================================================
   THE BAND OF LIVE FIGURES, TAKEN FROM COINGLASS AND NARROWED ON PURPOSE.

   Every CoinGlass page carries four numbers along the very top — 24h volume, open interest,
   liquidations, long/short — and that band is most of why the site reads as an instrument rather
   than as an article. It costs them almost nothing and it answers the question a reader arrives
   with before they have chosen a page.

   THREE THINGS ARE DIFFERENT HERE, and each is the reason to copy it rather than admire it.

   IT DOES NOT GO EVERYWHERE. Theirs appears above the documentation and the login screen, where
   the total open interest of the market is noise. This is rendered by the pages that are ABOUT
   the market and by no others: an explainer about what a funding rate is does not get a ticker.

   IT COSTS NO EXTRA READ. getSnapshot() is a raw KV get with no per-request memo, so putting
   this in the layout would have doubled the reads on every page in the site. The figures are
   props instead, derived from the snapshot the page has already fetched — a reduce over fifty
   objects, no I/O, and impossible to render against a different reading than the page's own.

   IT DOES NOT SHOUT. Theirs colours every delta green or red and pairs them with arrows, which
   is four decisions competing with the page underneath. These are mono figures on a hairline,
   faint labels, no badges, and colour ONLY where a number is genuinely signed — which on this
   site means the funding pair and nothing else.
   ========================================================================================= */
interface Item {
  label: string;
  value: string;
  /** Signed values take the funding pair; leave unset for magnitudes. */
  tone?: "l" | "s";
  href?: string;
}
interface Props {
  items: Item[];
}
const { items } = Astro.props;
---

{items.length > 0 && (
  <div class="strip">
    {items.map((it) => (
      <div class="strip__cell">
        <span class="strip__label">{it.href ? <a href={it.href}>{it.label}</a> : it.label}</span>
        <span class:list={["strip__value", it.tone === "l" && "pays-l", it.tone === "s" && "pays-s"]}>{it.value}</span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
)}

<style is:global>
  /* A HAIRLINE, NOT A BAR. The reference paints its band on a tinted ground with a border and
     sits it above the masthead; that reads as chrome and competes with the page title. This is
     the same information with the box taken away — figures on a rule, which is how a broadsheet
     prints a summary and why it stays quiet at the top of a document that has its own headline. */
  .strip {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--gap-block) var(--gap-region);
    margin: 0 0 var(--gap-block); padding-bottom: var(--gap-block);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
  }
  .strip__cell { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
  .strip__label { font-size: var(--fs-xs); color: var(--text-faint); }
  .strip__label a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
  .strip__label a:hover { color: var(--text); }
  /* Tabular figures, so a column of these does not shuffle sideways when a digit changes on the
     tick — the same reason every number in the tables below is set this way. */
  .strip__value { font-size: 17px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--text); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  /* THE PHONE KEEPS ALL OF THEM, and this is where the reference gives up: at 375px CoinGlass
     scrolls its band sideways off the screen, so a phone reader sees two of four numbers and no
     indication the others exist. Wrapping into two rows costs one line of height and loses
     nothing. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .strip { gap: var(--gap-block) var(--gap-block); }
    .strip__value { font-size: 15px; }
  }
</style>
